{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "The target mapping di_dt depends on q and i with a fixed dissipative law -(resistance + resistance_nonlinear_coeff*Abs(i))*i, so for fixed q the damping contribution is an instantaneous function of current only.",
      "child_relation": "Release the resistor's effective resistance from a fixed parameter to a slowly evolving thermal state, so di_dt depends on q, i, and the accumulated recent current history through self-heating; for the same instantaneous i and q, the slope can differ depending on prior dissipation.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "i",
        "q",
        "resistance"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "In larger-amplitude ring-downs the late-time decay becomes more strongly damped than early-time decay because heating accumulates, whereas the parent can only tie damping instantaneously to |i| and cannot create this history-dependent curvature change with one shared parameter set."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "joule_power",
        "expression": "(resistance + resistance_nonlinear_coeff*Abs(i))*i**2",
        "role": "Instantaneous dissipative power that would drive resistor self-heating"
      },
      {
        "name": "electromagnetic_energy",
        "expression": "q**2/(2*capacitance) + inductance*i**2/2",
        "role": "Stored circuit energy setting transient amplitude and accessible heating"
      },
      {
        "name": "current_scale_ratio",
        "expression": "resistance_nonlinear_coeff*Abs(i)/resistance",
        "role": "Measures when existing instantaneous nonlinear damping is important and helps separate it from slower thermal memory effects"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when Joule heating integrated over several oscillation or decay times is large enough to change effective resistance appreciably, while thermal relaxation is not so fast that resistance remains effectively instantaneous; practically, moderate-to-large initial stored energy and finite ring-down duration are required.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "Across trajectories with different initial charge but fixed component values, training already contains stronger dissipation for larger-amplitude releases; in holdout, the same mechanism extends to a distinct history effect: at matched instantaneous i and q reached at different times within a transient, the child predicts different di_dt because the resistor has warmed.",
    "continuation_path": "In the limit of negligible self-heating or infinitely fast thermal equilibration to a constant ambient value, the evolved resistance becomes effectively constant and the parent law is recovered.",
    "freedom_change": "parameter_to_state",
    "promoted_symbol": "resistance",
    "promotion_reason": "A fixed resistance cannot encode thermal memory. To make the target mapping history dependent under the same observed q and i, the effective resistance itself must evolve as a state driven by dissipation.",
    "gap_statement": "The model treats resistance as fixed apart from instantaneous current dependence, omitting resistor self-heating that changes damping over the course of the transient.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The circuit is series-connected with fixed resistance, inductance, and capacitance.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Electrothermal resistance drift: Joule dissipation heats the resistor, increasing its effective resistance on a finite thermal timescale during the free decay.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "i",
      "q",
      "resistance",
      "resistance_nonlinear_coeff"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the fixed-resistance assumption rather than add an unrelated new force. The parent's target mapping uses a single instantaneous damping law, so once q and i are specified, di_dt is fixed. That is a fixed-environment shortcut because the dissipative environment cannot evolve with absorbed energy. In practice, with different initial capacitor charges and all other component values held fixed, higher-energy releases dissipate more Joule power and can warm the resistor during the observed ring-down. Then the same instantaneous pair (q,i) reached later in a strong transient can produce a more negative di_dt than earlier or than in a weaker release, because the effective resistance has increased. A single refitted parent parameter set cannot reproduce this across conditions: increasing constant resistance to match late-time strong-run damping would overdamp weak runs and early-time strong-run segments, while increasing only resistance_nonlinear_coeff still leaves damping an instantaneous function of |i| and cannot distinguish equal |i| states with different thermal histories. The effect is scientifically admissible when heating is appreciable but not so extreme as to violate finite-state assumptions, and it may be weak for very small initial charge or very fast thermal relaxation."
  },
